1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,229
A new disk is blank. It cannot hold a single file, until you give it a file system.

2
00:00:05,249 --> 00:00:07,975
And there are three you must know for the exam.

3
00:00:08,445 --> 00:00:14,291
In this video: create, mount, unmount, and use V F A T, E X T four, and X F S.

4
00:00:18,811 --> 00:00:21,394
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:21,864 --> 00:00:27,689
Create, mount, unmount, and use VFAT, ext4, and XFS file systems.

6
00:00:28,159 --> 00:00:32,872
We'll make three partitions, put one file system of each type on them, mount them,

7
00:00:32,892 --> 00:00:35,008
write a file, and unmount them again.

8
00:00:43,528 --> 00:00:47,965
A file system is the structure that lets a disk store files and folders.

9
00:00:47,985 --> 00:00:51,679
A blank partition has none, so the system cannot use it yet.

10
00:00:56,149 --> 00:00:57,897
Three types matter today.

11
00:00:57,917 --> 00:01:02,423
V F A T works on almost every system, but it has no permissions.

12
00:01:02,443 --> 00:01:05,394
Ext four is the classic Linux file system.

13
00:01:05,414 --> 00:01:09,142
And X F S is the default on Red Hat Enterprise Linux.

14
00:01:15,612 --> 00:01:17,596
The cycle is always the same:

15
00:01:17,616 --> 00:01:22,293
make the file system with mkfs, mount it on an empty folder, use it,

16
00:01:22,313 --> 00:01:24,573
and unmount it when you are done.

17
00:01:32,093 --> 00:01:37,106
We start with a blank disk, vdb, five gigabytes, with nothing on it.

18
00:01:37,126 --> 00:01:39,326
L S B L K shows no partitions.

19
00:01:46,846 --> 00:01:51,988
First a G P T label, then three partitions of three hundred megabytes each.

20
00:01:52,008 --> 00:01:56,255
We name them fat, ext, and xfs, so we remember which is which.

21
00:02:01,725 --> 00:02:06,548
L S B L K shows vdb one, vdb two, and vdb three.

22
00:02:13,068 --> 00:02:14,850
Now the file systems.

23
00:02:14,870 --> 00:02:20,428
Mkfs dot vfat makes a V F A T file system on the first partition.

24
00:02:20,448 --> 00:02:24,461
Mkfs dot ext four makes ext four on the second.

25
00:02:24,481 --> 00:02:28,065
And mkfs dot xfs makes X F S on the third.

26
00:02:28,085 --> 00:02:32,184
We show only the first lines of the long output.

27
00:02:55,704 --> 00:03:01,040
L S B L K with dash f lists the file system on every partition.

28
00:03:01,060 --> 00:03:05,206
V F A T, ext four, and X F S. Each has a U U I D.

29
00:03:05,226 --> 00:03:08,777
Notice that this V F A T says FAT sixteen:

30
00:03:08,797 --> 00:03:12,943
for a small partition, mkfs picks that by itself.

31
00:03:23,463 --> 00:03:26,693
To use a file system, you mount it on a folder.

32
00:03:26,713 --> 00:03:31,742
We create three empty folders under slash M N T with mkdir, one per type.

33
00:03:40,212 --> 00:03:43,051
Then mount, the device first, then the folder.

34
00:03:43,071 --> 00:03:48,149
After the three mounts, D F with dash h T shows the type and the size of each one,

35
00:03:48,169 --> 00:03:49,578
mounted where we asked.

36
00:04:05,098 --> 00:04:06,086
Now we use one.

37
00:04:06,106 --> 00:04:11,529
We write the word hello into a file on the ext four file system, and ls shows it,

38
00:04:11,549 --> 00:04:15,830
next to lost and found, a folder every ext four file system has.

39
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:30,635
Findmnt shows how the X F S one is mounted: the device, the type, and its options.

40
00:04:40,155 --> 00:04:44,489
When we are done, we unmount. Umount takes the folder. Note the spelling:

41
00:04:44,509 --> 00:04:46,279
umount, with no N after the U.

42
00:04:53,749 --> 00:04:58,378
Findmnt on the ext folder now prints nothing, which means: not mounted.

43
00:04:58,398 --> 00:05:01,390
D F on that folder shows the root file system,

44
00:05:01,410 --> 00:05:04,140
because the folder is just a folder again.

45
00:05:15,660 --> 00:05:19,096
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

46
00:05:20,616 --> 00:05:25,227
We reboot. The three file systems are still there, with the same U U I Ds.

47
00:05:25,247 --> 00:05:28,856
They are not mounted any more, because we mounted by hand.

48
00:05:28,876 --> 00:05:32,799
We mount the ext four one again, and our file still says hello.

49
00:05:44,319 --> 00:05:48,272
Your turn. Partitions vdb one and vdb two exist.

50
00:05:48,292 --> 00:05:53,322
Make an X F S file system on vdb one, and V F A T on vdb two.

51
00:05:53,342 --> 00:05:59,034
Mount vdb one on slash M N T slash data one, and vdb two on data two.

52
00:05:59,054 --> 00:06:05,739
Write a file called ok dot txt on the first. Unmount the second. Run the checker.

53
00:06:13,209 --> 00:06:14,960
Try before you open the solution.

54
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:24,876
Next up: mount and unmount network file systems using N F S.

55
00:06:25,346 --> 00:06:28,249
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
